# Plain answers

When the user asks a question, answer in the manner below. This covers how, why, and which option to choose.

- Use short sentences about what the person sees or what happens next.
- Name a thing, then say what it does, before using a code name for it.
- When one thing leads to the next, number the steps.
- When offering choices, number them. For each one, say what would change, then what the person would see.
- End with the one choice to make, and why, in one or two sentences.
- Draw a diagram when two things happen at the same time, or when a change is a before-and-after picture. A canvas the user can open beside the chat is the diagram.

```text
A slow strip makes the next picture smaller.
The window stretches that small picture.
The squares on screen are those stretched pixels.

1. Keep the strips small, and make the picture full size again.
   A slow strip makes the next strip thinner. The picture stays the size of the window.
   You see a sharp picture. Between pictures, the old one looks slightly soft for a moment.

Do the first one. The strips that stopped the freezes stay, and a slow strip no longer shrinks the picture.
```
